  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1253 AM MDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nThis is around the time of year when those of us who have been in \nWyoming for a while start thinking about when the first snow is \ngoing to fly. I can remember a few snowstorms before this date, \nincluding a few damaging ones. That makes days like this one even \nmore precious. Today may be the perfect early autumn day. High \npressure will be over the area today. The result will be a cool, \ncrisp morning followed by a comfortably warm afternoon with lots of \nsunshine. Wind should also remain fairly light as well, especially \nby Wyoming standards. And, it falls on a weekend, so most people \nwill get to experience it. Enjoy!\n\nThings get a bit more active on Monday. A Pacific trough will begin \nto approach the area from the west. This will tighten the pressure \ngradient somewhat and bring a gusty breeze, especially to the \nfavored southwest flow areas from east of Rock Springs through \nCasper. This area has at least a 3 out of 4 chance of wind gusts \npast 30 mph according to the NBM ensemble forecast, along with \nareas like the Upper Wind River Basin and the Lee of the Absarokas. \nWith mean relative humidity on the NBM ensemble generally less\nthan 15 percent in these areas, we hoisted Fire Weather Watches\nfor this area from 12 pm to 6 pm Monday. However, elevated fire\nweather will be possible elsewhere. And, with the warming, \ndownsloping flow, it will be an abnormally warm day for the \nfifth of October. Most locations East of the Divide under 6000 \nfeet in elevation have at least a 3 out of 4 chance of high \ntemperatures over 80 degrees. Most locations under 5000 feet \nhave a 1 in 2 chance of highs of 85 degrees or higher. There \nwill likely be some daily record high temperatures. A few \nlocations could get close or possibly break all time monthly \nrecord high temperatures at well, which are generally in the mid\nto upper 90s.\n\nThe pressure gradient should relax somewhat on Tuesday, so the \nresult will be less wind and as a result some improvement in fire \nconditions, This should allow for temperatures to be a bit cooler, \nbut still well above average and possibly break some more daily \nrecords. The trough will be passing to the south on this day. There \nmay be just enough moisture for a stray shower or thunderstorm in \nSweetwater County in the afternoon, but the chance is less than 1 \nout of 8 so we left it out of the forecast for now.\n\nWarm and dry weather should continue through the remainder of the \nworkweek. The next chance for critical fire weather looks to be \nFriday as a shortwave trough moves through the area na could bring \nsome gusty wind and low humidity. We may have a pattern change \nfollowing this as the trough breaks down and a more potent Pacific \nsystem approaches from the west. It could arrive as early as \nSaturday, but experience tells me the models are usually too fast \nwith Pacific systems this far out, so it is more likely for Sunday. \nThis is not certain, but odds are increasing of seeing some cooler \nand possibly wetter weather for next weekend or early the\nfollowing week.",
